  "$comment_segmentOrder": "ORDER IS THE DEFAULT SUGGESTION. The picker shows the list as stored, so the sequence is a product decision: interior design and execution first (what most profiles are), then materials and sanitary supply, then the adjacent trades. Alphabetical would put Acoustics above Interior designer.",
  "$comment_categoryOrder": "Same rule as segments: interior industries first, then sanitary and plumbing, then building services; sectors keep their own group after.",
  "$comment_classifications": "TWO VALUES, derived and never stored. This module used to classify a user by their membership state — Active Member, Paused, Suspended, Past Member — and that classification moved out with the membership feature: a subscription is a Finance record now, and asking the user base who is paying would be two modules holding one fact. classify() in views/Users/store.ts is the single derivation and it reads user_status alone.",
  "$comment_metricDefinitions": "Conversion to a paid plan, renewal rate, churn, expiring-soon and revenue per member were defined here while this module owned memberships. They are asked of the subscription that holds the money now and are defined in Finance; a second definition here would be the disagreement this operation exists to remove.",
  "$comment_openDecisions": "Only the decisions this module can still take. UM-OD-01 to 06, 11, 12, 13 and 15 were all about the membership lifecycle, its plans, its pause and suspension rules and its revenue KPI; they left with the feature. Their subject matter is Finance's to decide now.",
  "$comment_eventTypes": "THE MEMBERSHIP_* TYPES ARE HISTORICAL AND THEY STAY. This module no longer writes any of them, but the audit is a history: rows recorded while it did run a membership lifecycle describe things that actually happened, and the shared admin audit log still holds them. Dropping their labels here would render those rows with a raw key or with a generic word that loses which lifecycle move it was — editing the past to match today's feature set. Nothing writes them; everything can still read them."
}
